{"tool_name":"protedna","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"protedna","home":"http://serv.csbb.ntu.edu.tw/ProteDNA/index.php","license":"Unlicense","summary":"Identifies the sequence-specific binding residues in a transcription factor, protein-DNA interaction.","addition_date":"2017-02-14T09:35:11Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T21:03:42.488032Z","tool_type":["Web application"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:protedna","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"protedna@mars.csie.ntu.edu.tw","typeEntity":"Project","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"note":"Tutorial material","type":["Training material"],"url":"http://serv.csbb.ntu.edu.tw/ProteDNA/document.php"},{"type":["General"],"url":"http://serv.csbb.ntu.edu.tw/ProteDNA/method.php"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Peak calling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3222"},{"term":"Residue interaction calculation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0248"},{"term":"Residue interaction prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0272"},{"term":"Transcription factor binding site prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0445"},{"term":"Transcriptional regulatory element prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0438"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"ProteDNA","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"ELIXIR-EE","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkp449","metadata":{"abstract":"This article presents the design of a sequence-based predictor named ProteDNA for identifying the sequence-specific binding residues in a transcription factor (TF). Concerning protein-DNA interactions, there are two types of binding mechanisms involved, namely sequence-specific binding and nonspecific binding. Sequence-specific bindings occur between protein sidechains and nucleotide bases and correspond to sequence-specific recognition of genes. Therefore, sequence-specific bindings are essential for correct gene regulation. In this respect, ProteDNA is distinctive since it has been designed to identify sequence-specific binding residues. In order to accommodate users with different application needs, ProteDNA has been designed to operate under two modes, namely, the high-precision mode and the balanced mode. According to the experiments reported in this article, under the high-precision mode, ProteDNA has been able to deliver precision of 82.3%, specificity of 99.3%, sensitivity of 49.8% and accuracy of 96.5%. Meanwhile, under the balanced mode, ProteDNA has been able to deliver precision of 60.8%, specificity of 97.6%, sensitivity of 60.7% and accuracy of 95.4%. ProteDNA is available at the following websites: http://protedna.csbb.ntu.edu.tw/ http://protedna.csbb.ntu.edu.tw/ http://bio222.esoe.ntu.edu.tw/ProteDNA.","authors":[{"name":"Cheng Y.-S."},{"name":"Chu W.-Y."},{"name":"Huang C.-C."},{"name":"Huang C.-K."},{"name":"Huang Y.-F."},{"name":"Oyang Y.-J."}],"citationCount":38,"date":"2009-08-04T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"ProteDNA: A sequence-based predictor of sequence-specific DNA-binding residues in transcription factors"},"pmcid":"PMC2703882","pmid":"19483101"}],"topic":[{"term":"Gene expression","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0203"},{"term":"Molecular interactions, pathways and networks","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0602"},{"term":"Protein folding, stability and design","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0130"},{"term":"Protein interactions","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0128"},{"term":"Transcription factors and regulatory sites","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0749"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"ProteDNA","home":"https://bio.tools/protedna","license":"Unlicense","summary":"Identifies the sequence-specific binding residues in a transcription factor, protein-DNA interaction.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openeben